   "id": "week-06-cidb-eligibility-jv",
   "kind": "lesson",
   "label": "Week 6",
   "sub": "CIDB Eligibility & JVs",
   "title": "Week 6 — CIDB Eligibility & JVs",
   "file": "lessons/week-06-cidb-eligibility-jv.md",
   "body": "# Week 6 — CIDB Eligibility, Status, Joint Ventures\n\n> **Assignment volume:** conduct **eligibility checks on 10 construction tenders**.\n> **Running total target:** 38 tenders.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why this week matters\n\nWriting is worthless if the client cannot legally bid. This week you become the person who answers *\"can we bid?\"* faster and more reliably than anyone else in the room.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Learning objectives\n\nBy Friday you can:\n- Verify a contractor's registration status on the Register and interpret the result.\n- Explain active vs suspended/lapsed status and its effect at closing *and* at award.\n- Explain potentially emerging status, upgrades, and track-record requirements.\n- Use the CIDB Joint Venture Calculator and explain the JV logic to a contractor.\n- Produce a written eligibility determination a client can rely on.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Core concepts\n\n### 3.1 How CIDB eligibility actually works\n\nGiven: *\"Tenderers must have a CIDB contractor grading designation of 5CE or higher.\"*\n\nCIDB's guidance is that public clients **specify** the grading requirement, should **only consider eligible contractors**, and must **verify** the contractor's status on the Register. So the buyer will check — your check must match theirs.\n\n**Source:** https://www.cidb.org.za/clients/procurement-guides/applying-register-of-contractors/\n\n### 3.2 The eligibility checklist\n\n| # | Check | Where | Fail consequence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Class of works matches the required class | Register record | Ineligible |\n| 2 | Grade ≥ required grade | Register record | Ineligible |\n| 3 | Registration **active** (not lapsed/suspended/expired) at closing | Register record | Ineligible |\n| 4 | Registration still valid at **award/verification** | Renewal date | Award risk |\n| 5 | CRS/registration number matches the entity bidding (exact legal entity) | CIPC + Register | Ineligible |\n| 6 | Tender value within the grade's value limit | Advertisement + guide | Ineligible |\n| 7 | Any specialist class separately required | Scope/advertisement | Ineligible |\n| 8 | Special conditions for lower grades (if the tender allows them) | Tender conditions | Conditional |\n| 9 | Not on a restricted/defaulters register | CSD + Treasury registers | Ineligible |\n| 10 | JV arrangement (if used) satisfies the tender's JV rules | Tender + CIDB JV rules | Ineligible |\n\n### 3.3 Concepts to master\n\n- **Grades** — financial capability bands tied to tender-value limits.\n- **Classes of works** — the discipline; a contractor may hold several, each with its own grade.\n- **CRS numbers** — the contractor's registration identifier; must correspond to the exact legal entity submitting.\n- **Active vs suspended** — registration must be current; renewals lapse silently and contractors often do not notice.\n- **Upgrades** — a contractor can apply to upgrade; assess realistically whether it can complete before closing. Never plan a bid around a hoped-for upgrade.\n- **Potentially emerging (PE) status** — a development mechanism allowing certain contractors to be considered for work above their grade under specified conditions; read the CIDB guide's current rules rather than relying on hearsay.\n- **Joint ventures** — combining partners' capability to meet a required designation, subject to CIDB's rules.\n- **Tender-value limits** — the ceiling per grade; cross-check against the tender's estimated value including VAT treatment as the tender defines it.\n- **Track record requirements** — evidence of completed works of a comparable nature/value, often required for grading and separately for functionality scoring.\n\n### 3.4 Joint ventures\n\nCIDB provides a **Joint Venture Calculator** as part of its construction-register services: https://jointventurecalculator.cidb.org.za/\n\nWhat you must be able to explain to a contractor:\n- A JV's combined designation is **calculated**, not simply the highest partner's grade.\n- The **class** requirement still has to be satisfied by the appropriate partner(s).\n- Tenders often impose their own JV conditions: signed JV agreement, lead partner, joint and several liability, percentage split, separate compliance documents **per partner** (CSD, tax, B-BBEE, LoGS).\n- B-BBEE and preference treatment of a JV is governed by the applicable rules and the tender — verify, never assume.\n- A JV doubles the administrative failure surface. Budget for it.\n\n**Drill:** run five realistic partner combinations through the calculator and record the outputs and your interpretation.\n\n### 3.5 The written eligibility determination\n\nYour deliverable format (client-facing):\n\n```\nELIGIBILITY DETERMINATION\nTender:            <no> — <title>          Closing: <date/time>\nRequired:          <designation> (source: advertisement / clause ref)\nClient entity:     <legal name, reg no>\nRegister status:   <grade/class, active?, verified on <date>, source>\nValue limit check: tender value <R> vs grade limit <R>\nVERDICT:           ELIGIBLE / NOT ELIGIBLE / ELIGIBLE SUBJECT TO <condition>\nReasoning:         <numbered, each with a source>\nOptions if not eligible: <JV / do not bid / upgrade feasibility>\nUnverified items:  <list — marked MISSING — CLIENT TO PROVIDE>\nPrepared by / date:\n```\n\nSign nothing you did not verify. \"Client told me\" is recorded as such.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Daily plan\n\n| Day | Study (2h) | Dissect (2h) | Produce (1h) | Review (1h) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Applying the Register — full read | Tenders 1–2 | Eligibility checklist v1 | Quiz on status types |\n| 2 | Status, renewals, suspension | Tenders 3–4 | 2 determinations | Challenge my verdicts |\n| 3 | PE status, upgrades | Tenders 5–6 | 2 determinations | Scenario drills |\n| 4 | JV rules + calculator | Tenders 7–8 | 5 JV calculations | Explain JV logic back to me |\n| 5 | Track record requirements | Tenders 9–10 | 2 determinations | Hostile audit |\n| 6 | Consolidate | Re-verify all 10 | 10 final determinations | Full exam |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Deliverables\n\n- 10 × written eligibility determinations\n- 1 × JV briefing note for contractors (one page, plain language)\n- 1 × eligibility checklist added to `templates/`\n- Tender Bible: CIDB eligibility section\n\n---\n\n## 6. Self-test (closed book)\n\n1. Your client holds 6GB and 4CE. The tender requires 5CE or higher. Verdict?\n2. Registration lapses two weeks after closing but before award. What do you advise?\n3. Why can a JV's combined grade not simply be assumed to be the lead partner's grade?\n4. Name five administrative items that must be supplied **per partner** in a JV bid.\n5. What is the correct response to \"we're upgrading to 6 next month, just bid anyway\"?\n6. Which register checks sit outside CIDB entirely?\n\n---\n\n## 7. AI drill prompts\n\n> Give me 10 CIDB eligibility scenarios with tricky details (lapsed registration, wrong class, JV, value limit near the boundary, specialist class). Ask me for a verdict and reasoning on each. Grade strictly and cite CIDB guidance for corrections.\n\n> Review my eligibility determination below as if you were the buyer's evaluation committee. List every assertion I made without a verifiable source.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Mastery gate\n\nTen written determinations produced, each in **under 15 minutes**, each with a source for every assertion, zero verdicts reversed on review.\n",
