   "id": "week-10-construction-compliance",
   "kind": "lesson",
   "label": "Week 10",
   "sub": "Construction Compliance",
   "title": "Week 10 — Construction Compliance",
   "file": "lessons/week-10-construction-compliance.md",
   "body": "# Week 10 — Construction Compliance, H&S and Supporting Evidence\n\n> **Assignment volume:** build the **contractor master-document checklist**.\n> **Running total target:** 55 tenders (keep dissecting packs daily).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why this week matters\n\nConstruction packs carry a second compliance layer on top of the general supplier documents. The competent practitioner knows these are **conditional** — the tender decides. The checklist copy-paster demands every document from every client and destroys their own credibility.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Learning objectives\n\nBy Friday you can:\n- Explain what a Compensation Fund Letter of Good Standing proves and when it is required.\n- State the conditional logic for NHBRC, PSIRA, professional registrations and sector accreditations.\n- Assemble a complete, tender-driven document checklist for any contractor.\n- Coordinate H&S documentation without pretending to be a safety specialist.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Core concepts\n\n### 3.1 COIDA and the Letter of Good Standing\n\nThe Department of Employment and Labour explains that employers using contractors should obtain a **Compensation Fund Letter of Good Standing** from those contractors, demonstrating registration and that assessment payments are up to date.\n\nWhat it proves: the contractor is registered with the Compensation Fund and its assessments are current.\nWhat to check: entity name matches the bidder exactly · expiry date vs closing date **and** likely award date · lead time to obtain or renew.\n\n**Source:** https://www.labour.gov.za/DocumentCenter/Pages/Compensation-Fund--obligations-of-the-employer-.aspx\n\n### 3.2 The conditional compliance universe\n\nYou may encounter any of these:\n\n**COIDA · UIF · NHBRC · professional registrations · trade certifications · PSIRA · municipal accounts (rates clearance / no arrears) · insurances · performance guarantees · health-and-safety documentation · sector accreditations**\n\n> **Never assume all of these are mandatory universally. The specific tender decides.**\n\n### 3.3 Conditional logic — the distinction that matters\n\n**Wrong:** \"Every construction company must submit NHBRC for every tender.\"\n\n**Right:** \"NHBRC may matter here because this is homebuilding.\"\n\nNHBRC states that anyone in the business of building homes is required to register and renew membership annually. So the trigger is the *nature of the work*, not the word \"construction.\"\n**Source:** https://www.nhbrc.org.za/why-register/\n\nBuild a trigger table in your Tender Bible:\n\n| Document | Trigger | Where the tender says so |\n|---|---|---|\n| Letter of Good Standing (COIDA) | Employer with employees; nearly all construction packs | Returnables list / conditions |\n| NHBRC registration | Home building work | Scope + returnables |\n| PSIRA | Security services element | Scope + returnables |\n| Professional registration (ECSA/SACPCMP/SACAP etc.) | Named professional roles required | Key personnel criteria |\n| Trade certifications | Specified trades (e.g. electrical CoC capability) | Specification |\n| Municipal accounts / rates clearance | Frequently required by municipalities | MBD / returnables |\n| Insurances (CAR, public liability, SASRIA) | Contract data requirement | C1 contract data |\n| Performance guarantee | Above a stated value threshold | Contract data |\n| Sector B-BBEE (construction sector code) | Where the tender specifies the sector code | Preference clause |\n\nEvery row must cite **this tender's** clause before you request the document from a client.\n\n### 3.4 Health and safety documentation\n\nTypical H&S items in a construction pack: OHS specification issued by the client; a required site-specific **health and safety plan**; baseline risk assessment; appointments (construction manager, safety officer, first aiders, etc.); proof of competence; incident statistics; letter of good standing; sometimes a priced H&S item in the preliminaries.\n\n**Your role:** coordinate and check completeness against the tender's list; identify who must produce each item; ensure the H&S plan is site-specific and aligned to your methodology. **A safety specialist produces and signs it.** Never draft a risk assessment as if qualified.\n\n### 3.5 The contractor master-document checklist\n\nYour week's core deliverable. A permanent, reusable register per client:\n\n| Document | Held? | Issuer | Number/ref | Issue date | Expiry | Lead time to renew | Location in file | Last verified |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CIPC registration docs | | CIPC | | | n/a | | | |\n| CSD registration summary | | CSD | | | rolling | | | |\n| SARS Tax Compliance Status (Good Standing PIN) | | SARS | | | live status | | | |\n| B-BBEE affidavit/certificate | | Commissioner/SANAS agency | | | | | | |\n| CIDB registration | | CIDB | CRS no. | | | | | |\n| COIDA Letter of Good Standing | | Compensation Fund | | | | | | |\n| UIF registration | | DEL | | | | | | |\n| NHBRC (if applicable) | | NHBRC | | | annual | | | |\n| Insurances | | Insurer | | | | | | |\n| Bank rating / financial statements | | Bank / auditor | | | | | | |\n| Key personnel CVs + certificates | | — | | | | | | |\n| Completion certificates / reference letters | | Clients | | | | | | |\n| Plant list + ownership/lease proof | | — | | | | | | |\n| Company profile | | — | | | | | | |\n| Signatory resolution / power of attorney | | Company | | | | | | |\n| Municipal account / rates clearance | | Municipality | | | | | | |\n\nMaintaining this register for a contractor is itself a sellable retainer service — and it makes every subsequent bid dramatically faster.\n\n### 3.6 The expiry calendar\n\nBuild a simple calendar of every expiring document per client, with reminders at 60/30/14 days. Most construction bids that die administratively die on an expiry date someone forgot.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Daily plan\n\n| Day | Study (2h) | Dissect (2h) | Produce (1h) | Review (1h) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | COIDA / Compensation Fund | Pack A returnables | LoGS notes | Quiz |\n| 2 | NHBRC + conditional triggers | Pack B returnables | Trigger table | Scenario drills |\n| 3 | Professional/trade registrations | Pack C returnables | Registration notes | Test me |\n| 4 | Insurances & guarantees | Pack D contract data | Guarantee/insurance notes | Risk discussion |\n| 5 | H&S documentation | Pack E OHS spec | H&S coordination checklist | Boundary check |\n| 6 | Consolidate | Cross-compare all 5 packs | Master-document checklist + expiry calendar | Full exam |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Deliverables\n\n- 1 × contractor master-document checklist (reusable, in `templates/`)\n- 1 × conditional trigger table\n- 1 × H&S coordination checklist with the scope boundary stated\n- 1 × expiry calendar template\n\n---\n\n## 6. Self-test (closed book)\n\n1. What does a Letter of Good Standing prove and who issues it?\n2. When is NHBRC relevant, and when is it not?\n3. Name six conditional compliance documents and their triggers.\n4. Where in a pack do you find insurance and guarantee requirements?\n5. What part of H&S documentation may you never produce yourself?\n6. Why is an expiry calendar a competitive advantage?\n\n---\n\n## 7. AI drill prompts\n\n> Here is a construction tender's returnables list. For each compliance document, tell me the clause that requires it and whether it is mandatory or scored. Then list any document I might *wrongly* assume is required but which this tender does not ask for.\n\n> Quiz me on conditional compliance triggers. Give me 10 project descriptions; I say which registrations are likely triggered and why. Correct me with official sources.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Mastery gate\n\nFor an unseen construction pack you produce a complete, clause-referenced document request list for the client in **under 30 minutes**, requesting nothing the tender does not require.\n",
